      "documentSlug": "pd-pain-fatigue-and-sensory-symptoms",
      "documentConcept": "PD pain, fatigue and sensory symptoms",
      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:b910e8016c1651674ba32b00149e6c40a24df2c32f7ad4267bb63084380a5417",
      "text": "Pain, fatigue and sensory disturbances contribute to disability in Parkinson\u2019s disease. The cited sources characterize pain by syndrome; sensory symptoms can involve peripheral neuropathy as well as altered perception. Fatigue is distinct from sleepiness; Parkinson\u2019s Foundation guidance describes assessing other causes, pacing activity and regular exercise. Treatment evidence is uneven: historical MDS ratings are qualified, PANDA and OXYDOPA did not establish their primary analgesic benefits, and sham-controlled acupuncture studies did not show a fatigue-specific advantage. Laboratory changes in pain thresholds should not be equated with durable clinical pain relief. Preliminary stimulation studies remain specific to the pain types and follow-up studied.",
